        '\n' +
        'Cart ruts ran deep and old toward the walls. Traffic thickened as he walked: a farmer with a loaded handcart, two women driving geese with sticks, a mule train led by a boy who whistled through his teeth. Nobody looked at him twice. A traveler in plain robes with a staff on his back was the kind of thing this road saw every morning.\n' +
        '\n' +
        "The walls grew as he came on—gray stone, patched in places with newer, paler masonry, high enough that their shadow reached the road before the walls themselves were close. The gate stood open, a double arch with the leaves drawn back, and a line of people and beasts shuffled through it in no particular hurry. Two guards in the city's colors stood under the arch, talking to each other more than watching anyone. Beyond the gatehouse, smoke rose from a hundred chimneys, and voices and hammer-blows carried thin on the wind.\n" +
        '\n' +
